============================================================================================
PART 1 -- THE RIDGE (wide-binary catalog: P, s, gamma_v) vs E6/E7
============================================================================================
  [PASS]   R-LEVEL gamma_v(s=10-30 kAU) plateau inside the E6/E7 surviving band [1.047, 1.11]
          measured : gamma_hat(10-30 kAU) = 1.0794 (band [1.047, 1.11]); +2.8 sigma from Newton
          sigma    : 2.8
          reading  : the LEVEL is triple-confounded and NOT the gating observable (G088: 'the ridge, not the level, is the identification's zero-parameter statement'): scored to the surviving band only.
  [PASS] R-RIDGE30.7 the P-s ridge at 10-30 kAU: sexc = s/s_N = 1.184 (+18.4%), the 30.7-sigma-class
          measured : sexc(10-30 kAU) = 1.1842 (+18.4%), sigma = 30.7 at N = 2500
          sigma    : 30.7
          reading  : the period-separation distortion: at fixed P the cloud pairs sit at s/sN = (1+m/M)^(1/3), +18.4% at the cap -- the identification's zero-parameter statement (E7).
  [PASS] R-BREAK7.4 the E7 break at 7.4 kAU: sexc RISES below the cap, FLAT above (kill = no break)
          measured : rise ln-sexc 0.134 over sub-break bins; plateau flatness sigma_ln = 0.0000; break bracketed in (6.77, 10.36) kAU around the cap 7.43 kAU
          sigma    : 4.5
          reading  : triples and Newton pop the same flat s-excess at 0 offset with NO break; the cloud's sexcess must rise with s and break at the 7.4 kAU cap -- the axis that suppresses the triples.
  [PASS] R-NOTFLAT the ridge RISES with s (d ln sexc/d ln s > 0): not mimickable by a flat triple/Newton ridge
          measured : sub-break log-log slope = +0.071 (+19.4 sigma); registered class rho = 0.35 ~ 20 sigma
          sigma    : 19.4
          reading  : a triple's extra mass does NOT grow with s: sexcess flat, slope 0, no break -- the P-s axis suppresses the level confounders at >10 sigma.

============================================================================================
PART 2 -- THE DOUBLE-MAP (vertical-z catalog: R0 surface-density profiles)
============================================================================================
  [PASS]        D1 the 30->100 pc dark-density fall 4.2x vs ~1.0x single-component (d ln rho/d ln z -> -2)
          measured : rho_dark(30)/rho_dark(100) = 4.05x (single-sech2 1.01x) = 0.61 dex ~ 6.0 sigma
          sigma    : 6.0
          reading  : the inner-region slope is slab-set (d ln rho/d ln z = -1.95 at 100 pc) and BREAKS at z_down; NFW and single-sech2 are flat to ~0 there -- the shape channel, systematics-limited.
  [PASS]        D2 the |z|<300 pc dark column 25.7-29.8 (central 27.8) vs 6.6 NFW -- ~4x
          measured : col_dark(|z|<300) = 25.9-37.9 (central 31.9) vs NFW 6.6 -> 4.2 sigma
          sigma    : 4.2
          reading  : the 26.7 Msun/pc2 slab column identity (a0/8piG, G024) dominates the 300-pc bin; the single-component alternatives carry only the smooth-halo column there (degrades < 2 sigma only if sigma_col >~ 10.5).
  [PASS]        D3 the NEGATIVE outer bin 180-562 pc: outer(300-2000)/inner(0-300) ratio NEGATIVE
          measured : outer/inner ratio = -0.45 (outer -14.3 Msun/pc2) vs +2.31 (sech2) / +5.57 (NFW) ~ 6.1 sigma sign separation
          sigma    : 6.1
          reading  : the sign of the outer-bin dark column is the cleanest single number: the two-scale map predicts the dark column DECREASES (net negative) between 300 and 2000 pc because the slab's negative layer (G024 V7, rho_dark < 0 on ~180-562 pc with the disk included) outweighs the sech2 disk; NO smooth single-component profile can produce a negative outer bin.  (F2 kill = single-scale / positive outer bin; F3 kill = positive dark mass at |z| ~ 300-560 pc.)

